Help for DRONE:

Manages the persistent drone scanner. Rules are matched against
each connecting user's nick, ident, IP and host; a match places a
K-Line on the user's IP (or host, if the IP is unknown).

Users who are logged in to services, IRC operators, services'
own clients and users covered by a DRONE EXEMPT mask are never
matched. New users are checked about two seconds after they
connect, so SASL and burst logins are seen.

A rule is either a wildcard mask (* and ?) or a POSIX extended
regular expression wrapped in slashes. Regexes are case-insensitive.
POSIX regex does not support \d or \w: use [0-9] and [a-z0-9_].

To check only one field, start the rule with nick:, ident: (or
user:), host: or ip:. Rules without a prefix check all four.

Durations: a number of seconds, or a number followed by one of
s, m, h, d, w, y (e.g. 10m, 1h, 7d). Use 0 or "perm" for a
permanent K-Line. The default is 24 hours.

Broad rules are refused: patterns with fewer than 3 letters or
digits (such as * or *a*), and rules that would match a large
share of the users online right now. Add FORCE before the mask
to add such a rule anyway.

Syntax: DRONE ADD [FORCE] <mask|/regex/> [duration] <reason>
        DRONE MOD <id> DURATION <duration>
        DRONE MOD <id> REASON <reason>
        DRONE DEL <id|mask>
        DRONE LIST [pattern]
        DRONE EXEMPT ADD <mask> <reason>
        DRONE EXEMPT DEL <mask|number>
        DRONE EXEMPT LIST
        DRONE TEST <nick|string>
        DRONE SCAN [EXEC]

Every rule has a permanent ID number, shown by DRONE LIST and in
K-Line alerts. IDs are never reused or renumbered, so deleting a
rule does not change the IDs of the others.

DRONE LIST with a pattern (wildcards * and ?) only shows rules
whose mask, reason or setter matches it, e.g. DRONE LIST *bot*.

DRONE EXEMPT masks protect users from every rule. A mask with
! or @ is matched against nick!ident@host and nick!ident@ip;
any other mask against the user's host and IP. CIDR ranges
such as 192.0.2.0/24 work too. Exemptions also apply to
DRONE SCAN and are left out of the broad-rule check.

DRONE TEST with the nick of an online user shows whether that
user would be K-Lined (and why, or why they are exempt); with
any other string it tests the string against every rule.

DRONE SCAN on its own only reports matches; DRONE SCAN EXEC
K-Lines every matching user currently online.

Each new K-Line is reported to the services log (e.g. a log
channel that includes "info"); during a flood, at most 5 per
10 seconds are shown, followed by a count of the rest.

Examples:
    /msg &nick& DRONE ADD nick:/^mybot[0-9]+$/ 7d Detected botnet
    /msg &nick& DRONE ADD host:*.badvpn.example perm Abusive VPN
    /msg &nick& DRONE ADD ip:192.0.2.* 1h Flood source
    /msg &nick& DRONE TEST mybot123
    /msg &nick& DRONE LIST *bot*
    /msg &nick& DRONE EXEMPT ADD 192.0.2.0/24 Webchat gateway
    /msg &nick& DRONE EXEMPT ADD *!*@bouncer.example Trusted bouncer
    /msg &nick& DRONE SCAN
    /msg &nick& DRONE MOD 1 DURATION 1h
    /msg &nick& DRONE DEL 1
